Firstmate fork amendments
=========================

Upstream documentation (docs/, README.md, AGENTS.md, skills) is kept
byte-identical to upstream and is never rewritten for this fork. Where this
fork behaves differently from what an upstream document says, the difference
is recorded here, per upstream document and section. Read this file before
trusting an upstream doc. The file has no extension on purpose (see
fork/README): upstream's documentation-audience check refuses unregistered
*.md, *.mdx, *.rst, and *.txt files.

Each entry names the upstream document and section, then what this fork does
instead. Add an entry when a deviation is created; remove it when the fork
catches up. Hook-block edits are not listed here - fork/README "The hook
points" owns those.


docs/configuration.md - "PR registration scan (config/pr-register-scan)"
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Upstream describes a scan that records reported PRs from done lines during the
fm-pr-check.sh sweep, enabled by config/pr-register-scan. This fork has no such
feature: bin/fm-pr-check.sh here has no scan path at all, so setting
config/pr-register-scan does nothing.

Two separate facts, both standing:
  - Lag: the section was added upstream by #8086 (9224c902), which this fork
    has not merged yet; its docs/ are byte-identical to the last upstream
    commit it did sync. The 15 docs files that differ from upstream/main are
    upstream's own newer changes, not fork edits.
  - Deviation: when the fork does sync #8086 the section will arrive, and it
    will still describe a feature the fork lacks unless the scan code is
    ported. Keep this entry until the code exists.


docs/scripts.md - the bin/fm-pr-check.sh scan row
-------------------------------------------------

Same as above: upstream lists a scan mode for bin/fm-pr-check.sh. This fork's
script has no scan path. The row is upstream-ahead (#8086) and, once synced,
documents a feature this fork does not have.
